<h2><a id="Presentació" name="Presentació"></a>Presentation <!--and options--></h2>
<p>This application allows quantifying the degree of spatial autocorrelation of a quantitative variable in a raster file by calculating an indicator, in this case the Moran's <i>I</i> (i) index. Contiguity is calculated at order 1 (the first ring of neighbors for each cell, equivalent to a 3x3 cell window).</p> 
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->

<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

<p>Spatial autocorrelation is a property present in the spatial distribution of a variable if the values at any location are influenced by values at nearby locations (following Tobler's first law of Geography <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobler%27s_first_law_of_geography"> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobler%27s_first_law_of_geography</a>). The concept of "closeness" will be referred to the autocorrelation threshold, limit distance where autocorrelation is very low or non-existent. This threshold is useful to impose the ideal spatial conditions on the sampling of the variable and certain associated statistical models.</p> 

<p>Moran's <i>I</i> index is calculated with the following formula:</p>
<p align="center"><img src="AutoCor.png"/></p>
<p>where:</p>
<p><img src="AutoCor1.png"/> is the number of spatial units, indexed by <i>i</i> and <i>j</i>;  <img src="AutoCor3.png"/> is the variable of interest; <img src="AutoCor4.png"/> is the mean of the variable of interest; <img src="AutoCor5.png"/> is an element of a matrix of spatial weights with zeros on the diagonal; <img src="AutoCor6.png"/> is the sum of all the elements of the weight matrix, that is:<br><img src="AutoCor7.png"/>.
<p>The weight matrix formed by the set of <img src="AutoCor5.png"/> it can be defined following the movement of chess rooks, or following the movement of the chess queen. In both cases the matrix assigns a weight of 1 to neighboring cells in the north, south, east and west directions. In the first case a weight of 0 is assigned to the other directions (diagonals), while in the second case a weight of 0.70711 (=1/&#8730;2) is assigned to the other directions.</p>

<p>Spatial autocorrelation makes it possible to identify spatial patterns other than purely random patterns and regular patterns, such as a chess board. In the example of the chess board, and applying the criterion of weights of 4 neighbors in a cross, the index obtained has a value of -1; on the other hand if the white checkers (squares) are stacked in one half of the board and the black ones in the other, the index approaches +1 as N increases; finally, in a random layout of the checkers the returned index will be a value close to 0. Values smaller than -1 or larger than +1 are possible, but usually correspond to highly skewed situations in the histogram and with some values with very few neighbors in relation to the distance analyzed.</p>

<p>NOTES:</p>
<ul>
<li>Calculating Moran's index requires variation in the data. If it is applied to a layer where all values are identical, the resulting index will be 0; however, in this case, concluding that the spatial distribution is random would be meaningless.</li>
<li>Spatial autocorrelation is scale-dependent. Therefore, it is expected that a generalized layer (for example with <href="CanviRes.htm">CanviRes</a> in contraction mode to obtain larger pixels based on a median criterion) provides different values.</li>
<li>This index should not be calculated for layers derived from rasters with larger cell sizes that have been converted into rasters with smaller cell sizes simply by subdividing the original cells (e.g., using the <href="CanviRes.htm">CanviRes</a> application in expansion mode), as the subdivided layer introduces a fictitious contiguity between cells.</li>
<li>When calculated using the 4-neighbor method on an image with a checkerboard pattern, the result is -1, as expected. Conversely, in 8-neighbor mode, autocorrelation increases significantly due to contact with the 4 diagonal neighbors; consequently, the index yields a value of -0.202709 for a 16x16 cell image (the fact that diagonal cells carry less weight prevents the value from being closer to zero).</li>
<li>An alternative to Moran's index is to use the Variability option of <href="Filtres.htm">Filters</a> application, which provides a local measure of variability for each convolution window. In addition to generating an indicator sensitive to the variation within different areas, if all the values of the raster result of Filters are selected with a query by attributes, the median or the mean of the result will give a general value of the layer.</li>
<li>To obtain an indicator similar to autocorrelation for images containing categorical variables, it is possible to follow this approach:</li>
	<ul>
	<li>Create a grid of regular polygons that tessellate the space. Then, using <a href="CombiCap.htm">CombiCap</a> tool in statistical field transfer mode, it is possible to calculate —among other metrics— the Shannon index for each polygon. The Shannon index is 0 for homogeneous regions and increases as the diversity of a region grows. In the resulting polygon layer, this index serves as a local indicator of diversity for each polygon. If all features are selected via an attribute-based query, the median or mean of the result will provide a general value for the layer.</li>
	<li>Using <href="RasSelec.htm">RasSelec</a> application, select the category of interest and specify that the output should be a mask (selected cells will be marked as 1), ensuring that 0 is not designated as NoData (unselected cells will be marked as 0). Next, run AutoCor using this result. Optionally, this process can be performed for all categories, after which the median or mean of the various resulting values can be calculated.</li>
	</ul>
</ul>

<p>More information can be consulted at the following reference:</p>
<b> Goodchild, M.F.</b> (1986) <i> Spatial Autocorrelation</i>. Concepts and Techniques in Modern Geography (CATMOG) n. 47, Geo Books, Norwich. 56 p. <a href="https://alexsingleton.files.wordpress.com/2014/09/47-spatial-aurocorrelation.pdf">https://alexsingleton.files.wordpress.com/2014/09/47-spatial-aurocorrelation.pdf</a> 

